00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:26.140
already from our last session

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:27.600
that I hope to get to some of them,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.100
but for any of y'all
on Facebook or YouTube,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.510
feel free to add questions.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.620
And we have team members
of Khan Academy who

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.670
are gonna be surfacing
them to myself and Angela,

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:38.270
but Angela, maybe a good place to start.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.450
You know, last time we talked

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.330
a lot about growth mindset and grit,

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:44.290
and it's kind of this, you know,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.010
you can easily get into this view that

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:48.110
like look, if you have a
growth mindset, you fail,

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.430
you hit some adversity,

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.480
you just keep chipping away at it.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.131
And eventually you'll
get to the other side.

00:02:53.131 --> 00:02:55.660
But how does that equate
with the other notion

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.610
that, you know, not everyone

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.020
has the same starting point,

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:02.110
not everyone the same
tools at their disposal.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:03.720
So how do you, how do you, you know,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.570
there's growth mindset
and grit and resilience

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.934
and then there's equity, really?

00:03:07.934 --> 00:03:10.730
Yeah, I have lately been, not just lately.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.360
I have been reading criticism of my work

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.200
and also Carol Dweck
work on growth mindset.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.380
And when I get past the
natural human reaction,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.100
which is total defensiveness
and I really listen,

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.821
I hear something that's really valuable.

00:03:25.821 --> 00:03:27.890
And I think the critiques are

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.500
that if you talk about grit, resilience,

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.320
and growth mindset and character,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.880
it sounds like it's all about the kid.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.270
It's all about the student

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.103
and the entire burden of responsibility

00:03:37.103 --> 00:03:40.290
falls on their shoulders.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.270
And there's no mention
of equity and poverty,

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:46.015
lack of opportunity, you
know, massive differences

00:03:46.015 --> 00:03:47.970
that we're all well aware exist,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.230
not only in our country,
but around the world.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.520
And I think the reconciliation is this.

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.090
It is not that these you
know, ideas about mindset

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.250
and learning and grit
are, I think at odds with,

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.790
you know, it's not an either or

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.960
like it's either the situation or

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.230
it's the person it's both and.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.220
And you know, very
briefly I'll just say so

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:11.910
that in psychology, there was like a war

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.117
between people in psychology,
psychological researchers

00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:16.310
who thought that, you know,

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.820
so much of our behavior was about

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.170
our personality and who we were.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.140
Then there were the other psychologists

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.490
who said, no, it's really
about the situation.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.560
And the war is over because
really everyone recognizes

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.260
that they both matter.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:32.290
I was once in a classroom,
in a public school

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:33.660
that shall remain nameless,

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:36.610
but these two boys were
sitting at their desk.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.010
All the other kids were doing their work.

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.190
They were all on these laptops, you know,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:43.300
pounding away at some assigned activity.

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.450
These two kids were doing nothing.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.090
Now, if you walked in,
just at that moment,

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.140
you would say, well, those
two kids don't have grit.

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.150
Those two kids are doing
nothing, but I walked

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.570
in actually before the bell rang.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.210
And I saw all the students walk up

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.320
to the laptop cart and these two boys

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.540
were last in line and they
got the two broken laptops.

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:01.010
They couldn't do anything all period

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.670
because they lacked opportunity.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:03.910
And I think that's the mistake

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.440
that I see critics not wanting to make,

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.035
not to mistake, lack of
opportunity for lack of grit.

00:05:10.035 --> 00:05:11.820
And I, you know, have to say

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.403
that I completely agree with that.

00:05:14.403 --> 00:05:15.236
That's stoplight.

00:05:15.236 --> 00:05:16.390
And your example's a good one.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:17.287
If you've got the broken laptop, I mean,

00:05:17.287 --> 00:05:19.300
that's an extreme example.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.030
Of course you're not gonna be able

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.367
to even apply your grit
or your growth mindset.

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:25.760
And it is fascinating how in many times

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.060
in academic circles people

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.450
can get into these religious calves,

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.760
but it's clearly not one of the other

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:34.510
to your point that
environment and opportunity

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:35.920
for sure is gonna make a difference,

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.222
but whatever set of cards you are dealt,

00:05:40.222 --> 00:05:41.950
if you are able to approach

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.040
that with a growth mindset,

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.761
given that context,

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:49.030
you're gonna get further, all else equal.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.010
That's not to say that
if two kids don't get

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.260
to a certain place or the same place

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.900
that you can somehow blame
the kid who didn't get there

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.180
on their lack of mindset,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:59.530
it might've been other things.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.090
It might've been a context.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:03.599
So yeah, it makes, it makes all the sense

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:08.070
in the world to me on
that, I mean you know,

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.750
just double clicking on that a little bit,

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:11.597
there's a question from Facebook

00:06:11.597 --> 00:06:14.600
Richie Getler asks.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.810
And I think a lot of
parents are wondering this

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:18.390
for at least one of their children.

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.040
How do you help inspire a kid who wants

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.680
to practice to want to practice something?

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:25.460
Is it essential that we simply do things

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.270
we like to do in order to be successful?

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:29.840
And we touched on about that a little bit

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.490
in our last conversation, but yeah.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.000
How do you motivate someone to practice

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.340
maybe something that is not obvious

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.050
that they love it, or
do you just say, well,

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:38.700
only do the things that you love

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.250
or how can you learn to love things?

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.700
- You know, since our last conversation,

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:43.990
which is now a few weeks ago,

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:45.790
so I've been thinking about what you said

00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:47.410
in that part of the conversation,

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:50.460
which is, you said that
pretty much anything

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.313
can become fascinating to you.

00:06:52.313 --> 00:06:54.100
And when you need to record a video,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.060
you'd like, you don't record

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:56.583
until you can become fascinated,

00:06:56.583 --> 00:06:58.870
but, but gosh, like what couldn't be,

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.046
you know what would fall
outside of that category?

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:03.290
And I've been thinking about it

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.830
because I wonder whether for you

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:08.400
as like who you are
your core as a teacher.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.570
And so what excites you is as being able

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:12.110
to understand something
and then being able

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:14.370
to teach it and communicate it.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.460
You can certainly tell me,

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.820
but let me respond first
to that question directly.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.460
I think that when kids are
starting out in something

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.240
that the, the thing to emphasize

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.410
is interest and enjoyment.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.610
And to wait a little bit if you can,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.710
to impose, you know, the hardest,

00:07:31.710 --> 00:07:35.440
most challenging repetitive
kind of practice.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.160
And that is because until

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.840
a child gets engaged in something,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.560
it's a very romantic period.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.540
In fact, some scientists use the term,

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.360
the romantic period to describe that

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.380
initial kind of flirtation.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:50.000
Like you kind of have a joy

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:50.833
to what you're doing,

00:07:50.833 --> 00:07:52.090
and then you love it so much

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.420
that you can evolve into somebody

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.253
who's willing to do more drills

00:07:55.253 --> 00:07:59.320
more like really hard stuff.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:02.280
Now with school, something
Sal that you have much more

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:03.800
experience with than I do.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.110
You might say that, you know,

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.890
for math for example, something,

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:10.020
I know that, you know
Khan Academy all started.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.560
It's not necessarily like,

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:13.820
if you think yourself like, well,

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:15.560
when is this romantic you gonna come?

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:18.730
Because you know, my kid
really doesn't love it.

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:20.850
I think that even

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.070
if you could just take
it as a principle, right?

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.350
Like how much enjoyment and interest

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.110
and fun can there be,

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:29.920
maybe it won't be as fun
as, you know Minecraft,

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:31.720
but like the more, the better,

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:34.310
and the earlier you
are in your development

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:36.910
as a learner, the more you
really need that I think.

00:08:37.815 --> 00:08:39.800
Yeah the way I think you are right.

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:42.670
I realized that my mindset
towards certain things,

00:08:42.670 --> 00:08:44.160
maybe isn't as mainstream as,

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:47.220
as we would like that we would all,

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.600
you know, I can get excited about

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:50.990
almost anything, you know,
this weekend you know,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.560
I'm Swiffer you know
wet Swiffering the house

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:55.911
and I'm fascinated by it.

00:08:55.911 --> 00:08:56.744
(laughing)

00:08:56.744 --> 00:08:58.700
(mumbles)

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.240
Yeah I love it, I love it.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:02.240
So, you know I do try to convince myself

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.610
that if there's something I'm gonna do,

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.890
I might as well enjoy it.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.910
I might as well appreciate

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.210
what I'm gonna get out of it.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.300
And, you know, a good example,

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:11.740
you know, kids for the most part,

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:14.300
don't complain if they're working out

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.550
at the gym and they
gotta run on a treadmill

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:18.790
for 30 minutes, or you know they're trying

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:19.623
to weight lift,

00:09:19.623 --> 00:09:21.760
and they're doing these
really weird exercises

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.300
that work on their forearms

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:25.770
or something like that,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:28.040
because they know that it's all,

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.740
well first of all, they
oftentimes will enjoy it.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:30.573
'Cause they're like,

00:09:30.573 --> 00:09:32.100
hey, I'm working on something,
I'm building a habit.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:33.190
That's going to make me better

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:34.350
in some way more prepared

00:09:34.350 --> 00:09:37.270
for battle with the world
or understand the world,

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.560
or be able to engage with the world

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:39.773
in a stronger way.

00:09:39.773 --> 00:09:42.130
So that's my gut sense
is that the more people

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:43.302
just appreciate that,

00:09:43.302 --> 00:09:45.626
the more that even some of the, you know,

00:09:45.626 --> 00:09:47.100
little hairy things that you have

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.520
to deal with in academics,

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:49.950
you can kinda power through them.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.560
'Cause you're like, "oh, this
is gonna make me better."

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:55.150
At being a holistic person,

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.460
you know, my son is,

00:09:57.460 --> 00:09:58.470
you know takes piano lessons

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.750
and he loves playing piano and famously.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.390
When you play piano, you
have to practice your scales

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:05.460
- Right
- And scales in a vacuum.

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.960
You know, just going up and down there,

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.360
that's obviously not composing.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.770
You're obviously not gonna make friends

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:12.672
at a party by just playing your scales.

00:10:12.672 --> 00:10:15.280
It might not be particularly that fun.

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:17.250
But when you recognize that by building

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:18.970
that muscle memory and getting better

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:19.870
and better at your skills

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:23.346
and even professional pianist
practice their scales,

00:10:23.346 --> 00:10:26.370
it, the is actually kind of become fun

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:27.310
and become interesting.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:30.260
And you know, you're becoming
better every time you do them.

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.790
- Yeah and I think you're
reminding us so that,

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.100
you know, what's interesting and fun.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.150
Isn't just objectively
what the activity is.

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:38.470
There are piano teachers,

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:41.067
I specifically know of one named Cindy Lam

00:10:41.067 --> 00:10:44.900
in Pasadena, California,
who makes scales fun.

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.530
She makes games out of them.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:49.110
Her students actually enjoy doing scale.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:51.710
So it's not just like, is it enjoyable

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.946
or not enjoyable, but it's in
a way like how it's framed.

00:10:54.946 --> 00:10:56.350
You know, one thing

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:58.000
that makes things really boring

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.070
for kids is when we don't give

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.383
them a purpose or a point to it,

00:11:01.383 --> 00:11:04.010
like do this worksheet, do this.

00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:04.843
Why?

00:11:04.843 --> 00:11:05.676
Just do it.

00:11:05.676 --> 00:11:06.950
You're not giving the student

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:08.980
any reason to feel like this activity

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.210
is connected to any larger goal.

00:11:11.210 --> 00:11:13.280
And actually there is a science of boredom

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:14.420
and one of the ways things

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.970
can be bored and boring is because

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:19.602
it lacks any obvious purpose.

00:11:19.602 --> 00:11:21.403
Another, and I actually just wrote

00:11:21.403 --> 00:11:25.020
about this and I actually
mentioned Khan Academy

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.660
specifically as an antidote to this.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.610
So one other way to be bored.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.120
So I just said, one way to be bored

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:32.730
is like, what's the point of this?

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:36.640
Another way to be bored if
things are too easy or too hard.

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:40.190
And either of those create
the psychology of boredom

00:11:40.190 --> 00:11:41.900
and the reason I mentioned Khan Academy

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:42.733
and this little essays,

00:11:42.733 --> 00:11:45.470
is because I think this is
why among other reasons,

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:46.900
Khan Academy is so great

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.300
because it allows students to self

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:53.300
kind of calibrate and also
through your backend algorithms

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:54.880
to deliver to students like

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:57.100
what they need in their sweet spot.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.490
But anyway, as as
parents and as educators,

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:01.330
I think the principle is, you know,

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:03.930
can you make things more
enjoyable and interesting.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:06.380
Can you you know give
kids the bigger picture?

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:08.620
Like this is why we're
doing these pushups,

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:10.230
or this is why we're doing scales.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:11.660
And also can you calibrate?

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:15.003
So the things are neither
too easy nor too hard.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.233
- Yeah and I have to remind
myself that all the time.

00:12:18.233 --> 00:12:19.210
I mean, especially with,

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:20.700
I've talked to others livestream,

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.740
our older too in my 11
year old and eight year old

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:23.573
have for the most part

00:12:23.573 --> 00:12:26.440
have been quite good in
this socially distance,

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.150
remote learning, whatever
you wanna call it world

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:29.550
they've been, but our five-year-old

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:31.300
has been difficult and it's very easy

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:33.430
to fall into that frustrated parent trap

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.430
of like, look, you just need
to do this one worksheet.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:37.700
You just need to do this one exercise

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:38.533
and it, you know.

00:12:38.533 --> 00:12:39.366
But they kinda get entrenched

00:12:39.366 --> 00:12:42.153
because and then if you just,

00:12:42.153 --> 00:12:42.986
and then, and then you both escalate

00:12:42.986 --> 00:12:44.100
(laughing)

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:44.933
at some point, you're like
giving them a time out.

00:12:44.933 --> 00:12:46.090
- Yes frequently.

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:48.310
- 'Cause they're, they're screaming at you

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:49.350
or they, you know,

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.350
and I've had to learn some oftentimes

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:53.710
the hard way you just have to sometimes

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:56.437
take a step back and say,
"okay, how do I put this

00:12:56.437 --> 00:12:59.397
"in a context that in this case,

00:12:59.397 --> 00:13:02.447
"my youngest, my son will have fun.

00:13:02.447 --> 00:13:03.940
"Like, can we turn this into a game?"

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.898
Does it not fit even a five-year-old

00:13:05.898 --> 00:13:08.910
will rebel if they feel
like they're being forced

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:13.480
or coerced versus if they
are invested in the project.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:14.510
And even if they don't see, you know,

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:16.440
they, you know, it's hard
to convince a five-year-old

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:17.925
that like, "hey, by writing better,

00:13:17.925 --> 00:13:19.477
"you know you're gonna have

00:13:19.477 --> 00:13:21.177
"this ability to communicate in the world

00:13:21.177 --> 00:13:23.437
"and you're gonna have to do
that the rest of your life."

00:13:23.437 --> 00:13:26.121
But even even just to say, you know,

00:13:26.121 --> 00:13:28.030
it's building a muscle, it's fun.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:28.863
It's a game.

00:13:28.863 --> 00:13:31.430
I think, I think is a start.

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:33.660
So one thing I forgot to introduce you

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:38.170
but people saw you all
your title on the screen is

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:40.010
that you have started

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.460
something called the Character Lab.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.600
And many people here have asked about

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:45.433
it from YouTube.

00:13:45.433 --> 00:13:47.060
Sean Numba what is Character Lab?

00:13:47.060 --> 00:13:48.810
James Lou question for Angela,

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.254
a bit about Resources and Character Lab.

00:13:50.254 --> 00:13:52.491
Arcade Jack, what is Character Lab?

00:13:52.491 --> 00:13:56.700
So people wanna know Angela,
what is Character Lab?

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:58.530
- Thank you for those three questions

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:02.280
because I'm so in love with Character Lab.

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.724
It's a nonprofit I
started with two educators

00:14:04.724 --> 00:14:08.780
and its mission is to
advance scientific insights

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.440
that help kids thrive.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:13.119
Now character is a word that Aristotle

00:14:13.119 --> 00:14:16.173
and Maria Montessori and
Martin Luther King use.

00:14:16.173 --> 00:14:19.890
But I think commonly it's called
social, emotional learning

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:23.100
or 21st century skills,
call it whatever you want.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.410
The list is the list that all parents

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:27.330
and educators want kids to develop

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:32.100
gratitude and curiosity,
empathy, social intelligence,

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:33.667
grit, growth mindset.

00:14:33.667 --> 00:14:36.960
There's a science behind
any of those things

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:37.793
that I mentioned.

00:14:37.793 --> 00:14:40.940
And I think the idea that
we're living in exciting times,

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:43.890
that rather than just
sermonizing to our kids

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:46.220
about these things, that
we hope they'll develop,

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.759
we can actually draw on
random assignment experiments

00:14:48.759 --> 00:14:52.110
that, you know, show how
to improve growth mindset

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.190
and ditto for gratitude, et cetera.

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:55.790
So if you go to that website,

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.810
it's all like Khan Academy,
philanthropically supported.

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:02.230
So if you are a parent or
an educator in particular,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:03.750
I hope that you find, you know,

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.580
direct dialing scientists
through this website as it were.

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:08.290
Because every word on that website

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:11.893
is written by a scientist,
not by an intermediary.

00:15:13.316 --> 00:15:14.630
- And I highly recommended it,

00:15:14.630 --> 00:15:16.190
we've used the work at Character Lab.

00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:17.970
I look at it sometimes I reference myself.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:21.160
As family and you know we
have a Lab School we started

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.630
and we've leveraged a lot
of the work at Character Lab

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:24.540
to think about,

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.490
'Cause one of the things we
try to do with the Lab School

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:29.160
is not just kids are
developing academically,

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:32.010
but social emotional development
is a very explicit part.

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:34.460
And we've used a lot of the
taxonomies and frameworks

00:15:34.460 --> 00:15:35.810
from the character lab to think about,

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:38.190
how do we even, you know,
what dimensions matter

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:39.290
and how do we even measure

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:41.900
or think about measuring
some of these things?

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:44.750
So from YouTube, we have a teacher here,

00:15:44.750 --> 00:15:47.537
Daniel Kinross asks,

00:15:47.537 --> 00:15:49.627
"how can teachers encourage grit

00:15:49.627 --> 00:15:51.307
"during remote learning

00:15:51.307 --> 00:15:52.797
"with students out of reach.

00:15:52.797 --> 00:15:56.300
"It seems easier than ever
for students to give up."

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:58.983
- Ah this is this is a question

00:15:58.983 --> 00:16:00.640
that is near and dear to my heart.

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.690
I have an 18 year old and
a 17 year old at home.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:06.090
Little, a little bit farther
down the track than you.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.180
So in terms of how old my kids are,

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:10.580
but you know, when there's
not only distance learning,

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:12.590
but there's also this
like very blurry line

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:14.270
between morning, afternoon,

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.846
and evening between weekday and weekend.

00:16:16.846 --> 00:16:19.500
It's just very easy for like suddenly

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:21.190
like nine days to slip away

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:23.920
and like nothing really got done.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:25.283
So it's a terrific question.

00:16:25.283 --> 00:16:28.050
I think the person that
I've turned to the most

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:29.410
during this distance learning

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:31.490
period for wisdom is Ron Berger.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:34.383
And so you know, the
expeditionary learning schools

00:16:34.383 --> 00:16:38.569
and Ron in particular as
the chief academic officer

00:16:38.569 --> 00:16:40.601
at such a siege on these things.

00:16:40.601 --> 00:16:43.150
And I think what his advice would be

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:45.870
is to actually take the
opportunity of distance learning

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:49.330
and this, you know, enforced strangeness

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:50.820
that we have as educators.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:53.070
And, and use it to move more aggressively

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:55.010
toward project-based learning.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:57.200
Toward things where like, you know,

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:58.910
it's not just like, do this worksheet

00:16:58.910 --> 00:16:59.780
or fill out this thing.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:01.940
It's like, we have a project,

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:03.830
like we're gonna try to
figure out, you know,

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.218
how to you know,

00:17:05.218 --> 00:17:08.260
make like recycling work
in this neighborhood,

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.540
or you could do something
it's very, COVID specific.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:11.840
Like there are lots of, you know,

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:13.007
older people in our neighborhood.

00:17:13.007 --> 00:17:15.060
How can we take on as a project?

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:16.802
Like getting them, their groceries.

00:17:16.802 --> 00:17:18.480
Now, some of the stuff you're thinking,

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:19.652
like how does this have to,

00:17:19.652 --> 00:17:21.700
you know, what does it have
to do with like reading,

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:23.000
writing and arithmetic?

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:25.250
And I think there, it's not easy

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:26.610
to like always draw the line,

00:17:26.610 --> 00:17:27.750
but that's the challenge.

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:30.930
In other words, when you do
projects that are authentic,

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:33.000
you do have to do a little
more work as an educator

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.860
to think how you're
going to integrate some

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:37.570
of the basic skills that
we need students to learn.

00:17:37.570 --> 00:17:39.600
But the tilt I think is really powerful

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.160
because when you do have a project

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:42.370
that you're excited about,

00:17:42.370 --> 00:17:44.233
you're not like dragging yourself

00:17:44.233 --> 00:17:47.360
out of bed to force yourself to do it.

00:17:47.360 --> 00:17:48.247
You're excited to do it.

00:17:48.247 --> 00:17:49.730
And you're kind of about it,

00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:51.040
frankly, like all the time.

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.480
So to learn more, I think

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:55.091
if you Google expeditionary learning

00:17:55.091 --> 00:17:57.350
and Ron Berger, you'll find

00:17:57.350 --> 00:17:59.800
that he's writing a lot
of wise things right now.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:02.120
- And you bring up an
interesting point here.

00:18:02.120 --> 00:18:04.460
We talked about these kind of camps,

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.220
these dogmatic camps that sometimes form

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:08.130
and academic circles before
we were talking about.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.490
You know, is it about context and equity

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:11.562
or is it more about mindset?

00:18:11.562 --> 00:18:13.480
And to your point, it's not an either or

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.760
it's both matter.

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.360
And you know, I've seen a similar

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:19.670
'cause we were talking about
project-based learning.

00:18:19.670 --> 00:18:22.520
I've seen a similar
type of dogmatic battle

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:24.080
in education circles where there's,

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:25.480
I guess you could say the traditionalist,

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.037
who would say "no, you've
gotta learn those skills.

00:18:28.037 --> 00:18:30.097
"You gotta learn, you
know, practice your scales,

00:18:30.097 --> 00:18:31.719
"so to speak before you
become a great pianist."

00:18:31.719 --> 00:18:32.552
- Yeah.

00:18:32.552 --> 00:18:34.970
- And then you would have
the more you could say,

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:38.144
progressive educators, project
based oriented program.

00:18:38.144 --> 00:18:41.400
Who're like, no, you should
start composing on day one.

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:42.870
So it becomes interesting.

00:18:42.870 --> 00:18:43.828
(laughing)

00:18:43.828 --> 00:18:46.190
But, but what I heard you say is

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:47.752
it still is a bit of a boat.

00:18:47.752 --> 00:18:51.220
You shouldn't go,

00:18:51.220 --> 00:18:52.600
you shouldn't, it's not all or nothing

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:54.307
on one side or the other.

00:18:54.307 --> 00:18:55.310
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:56.143
You know, both there,

00:18:56.143 --> 00:18:58.220
there is pretty much the
answer to so many questions.

00:18:58.220 --> 00:19:01.730
And I think that is because
there are trade offs, right?

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.410
And in project-based learning,
you get certain advantages.

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:06.060
But you know, maybe you don't get the,

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.380
you know, super you know,

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:11.960
densely packed, like you know,
repetitive skill part right.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:13.520
But then when you just do that,

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.700
then you maybe lose a little bit

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:16.710
of the intrinsic
motivation and the purpose.

00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:17.990
And you were just talking about how

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:19.530
that can be really demotivating.

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.490
So I think with project based learning

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:23.000
and maybe more traditional instruction,

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:24.920
I mean, I'm not, I'm not a expert

00:19:24.920 --> 00:19:26.310
or veteran educator,

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.390
but it does seem to me
that they are complimentary

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:30.730
and when you see the best schools

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:33.316
and the best classrooms and frankly like,

00:19:33.316 --> 00:19:35.350
you know, just the best
learning environments

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:36.540
more generally there,

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.710
there tends to be both rigor and autonomy.

00:19:39.710 --> 00:19:41.910
There tends to be, you
know, both creative projects

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.098
and like a systematic approach.

00:19:44.098 --> 00:19:46.896
And this is again why it's not easy to do,

00:19:46.896 --> 00:19:49.497
but I do think it's what
we're all aiming for.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:51.630
- Yeah, completely agree.

00:19:51.630 --> 00:19:53.810
You know, just using a sports analogy.

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.186
If you're a football player,

00:19:55.186 --> 00:19:58.460
you obviously have to
practice playing football.

00:19:58.460 --> 00:19:59.527
Like you have to play in games

00:19:59.527 --> 00:20:01.560
like that are real games
- Yeah.

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:02.940
- And that's kind of the projects

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:06.230
but you'll also do
sprints and weightlifting

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.310
and, you know, drills
catching or throwing a ball,

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:10.870
you know, you know, just
different random exercises

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:11.740
that feel a little bit,

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:13.984
and you you'll perfect those exercises.

00:20:13.984 --> 00:20:15.210
And once again, if you just did that,

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.360
you'd be like, what's the point.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.010
But when you know that, hey,
this is making me more prepared

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:22.790
for a game and I may or may
not use some of these skills

00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:24.070
in a given game,

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:26.850
but it's useful toolkit
for the rest of my life.

00:20:26.850 --> 00:20:28.460
Then all of a sudden that
too can be motivating.

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:29.410
And that's what I tell kids.

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:30.390
I was like, look, you know,

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:32.760
I'm not sure whether you're gonna have to

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:35.632
use this method for factoring a quadratic

00:20:35.632 --> 00:20:37.930
at in some point in the next five years.

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:39.464
I am genuinely not sure,

